=== PARITY (vs web ConflictWarnings.tsx) ===
  Data source -> bound via the shared state-holder seam (ConflictWarningsSource ->
    ConflictWarningsModel). The web component is a pure presentational leaf fed by its parent
    (AutomationBuilderPage) `conflicts: AutomationConflict[]` prop, computed from a validation
    request. The native source snapshot carries that parent-query phase + freshness/connectivity;
    the view performs NO HTTP. useTranslation -> P1/S10 CWStrings facade.
  Projection -> ConflictWarningsProjection.rows is a 1:1 port of `conflicts.map((c, i) => …)`:
    list key `${automation_id}-${index}` (preserved so repeated ids stay distinct), severity
    passthrough, and the body `"${automation_name}": ${reason}` composed byte-for-byte. The
    severity → variant/icon mapping reproduces the web ternary EXACTLY: `severity === 'warning'`
    -> warning tone + AlertTriangle (exclamationmark.triangle.fill); otherwise -> info tone +
    Info (info.circle.fill). Unknown wire severities decode to .info (the web else branch).
  Composition -> each row is a tinted, bordered banner mirroring the shared TSAlertBanner
    (@/components/feedback counterpart): leading severity SF Symbol in tone color, the constant
    localized title (automations.builder.conflict = "Potential Conflict"), and the verbatim
    `"{name}": {reason}` detail (user data, rendered Text(verbatim:) — not a catalog key).
  Shared components -> @/components/feedback maps to TSAlertBanner (banner), TSSkeleton (loading),
    ContentUnavailableView (empty, the primitive TSEmptyState wraps), and the TSErrorDisplay/
    TSQueryError + TSBadge looks (error retry + stale/offline chips). The banner/chrome resolve
    their strings through the per-surface P1/S10 table, so they are mirrored (not the literal
    LocalizedStringKey components, which resolve against the default catalog) — the 0039 precedent.
    Recharts -> none / Leaflet -> none (no chart/map on this surface).
  States (all render) -> the web leaf returns null when conflicts.length === 0; the native surface
    owns the full P4 states contract around the parent conflict-detection query:
      loading -> two redacted TSSkeleton banner rows (initial conflict check);
      empty   -> friendly ContentUnavailableView ("No conflicts detected") — the never-a-blank-box
                 native treatment of the web `return null` (the healthy no-conflict outcome);
      error   -> the parent-query failure (TSQueryError equivalent + a native retry -> source.refresh);
      conflicts -> the faithful web banner list;
      stale   -> a "Conflict check may be out of date" chip + a single auto-refresh on each rising
                 edge into the stale window (unit-tested);
      offline -> a "showing last known conflicts" chip over the cached list.
    Each state has a #if DEBUG preview + a unit test. The stale/offline/loading/error chrome is the
    native states-contract treatment of the parent query's lifecycle, not invented data branches.
  i18n -> the ONE web-source key (automations.builder.conflict) resolves through the P1/S10 facade
    with the web English fallback (coverage 1/1). Plus 9 native chrome/a11y keys (severity words,
    empty title/message, loading, error message, retry, stale, offline). No English literal in
    Swift (Text(verbatim:) over the facade; inline English lives only in the CWText fallbacks).
  a11y -> each banner is one .ignore element with a composed VoiceOver label (title + severity word
    + detail); the leading icon is hidden; the retry button + each status chip carry an
    accessibilityLabel; the loading rows are one labeled element; Dynamic Type via tokens; Reduce
    Motion gates the TSSkeleton shimmer.
  Telemetry -> ConflictWarningsModel.start() emits viewOpened(surface:"ConflictWarnings") -> bridges
    the shared Telemetry.track (P1/S11). Asserted by a spy test (fires exactly once, idempotent start).

